```java
public interface ConnectionPool<T> {
    T borrow() throws TimeoutException;
    void release(T connection);
    void close();
}
```

`borrow()` can throw a `TimeoutException` — it's not guaranteed to succeed immediately, which is itself a design statement: exhaustion is a normal, expected condition the caller must handle, not a bug.

```java
public class BlockingConnectionPool implements ConnectionPool<Connection> {
    private final BlockingQueue<Connection> idle = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();
    private final AtomicInteger created = new AtomicInteger();
    private final int maxSize;

    public Connection borrow() throws TimeoutException {
        Connection c = idle.poll();
        if (c != null) return c;
        if (created.incrementAndGet() <= maxSize) return createConnection();
        created.decrementAndGet();
        c = pollWithTimeout();
        if (c == null) throw new TimeoutException("pool exhausted");
        return c;
    }
}
```

A blocking queue gives thread-safe hand-off for free: `borrow` pulls from it, `release` pushes back to it, and the queue itself handles the concurrency of multiple threads borrowing and releasing at once.

```java
public void release(Connection c) {
    if (isHealthy(c)) {
        idle.offer(c);
    } else {
        c.closeQuietly();
        created.decrementAndGet();
    }
}
```

Checking health on release, not just periodically in the background, catches a connection that died mid-use immediately, instead of handing the same broken connection to the next borrower and failing their request too.
